1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,227
November fourteenth, two thousand four. Off the coast of San Diego.

2
00:00:04,227 --> 00:00:07,059
Commander David Fravor takes off from the U.S.S.

3
00:00:07,059 --> 00:00:10,599
Nimitz to investigate something on radar that shouldn't be there.

4
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:17,651
What he finds is a smooth, white, forty foot object. No wings. No engines. No exhaust.

5
00:00:17,651 --> 00:00:20,980
Hovering over the ocean like it's sitting on glass.

6
00:00:22,099 --> 00:00:27,820
He flies toward it. It mirrors him. Then, in seconds, it's gone.

7
00:00:28,966 --> 00:00:32,443
"I think it was not from this world," Fravor said later.

8
00:00:32,443 --> 00:00:34,339
"I'm not crazy, haven't been drinking.

9
00:00:34,339 --> 00:00:38,448
After eighteen years of flying, this was nothing close to anything I've seen."

10
00:00:39,566 --> 00:00:44,186
Nineteen years later, the U.S. government finally admitted it takes this seriously.

11
00:00:44,186 --> 00:00:46,880
Not with a cover-up. With a report.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:58,031
This is what that report actually says, what the real videos actually show, and what a whistleblower told Congress under oath, that the government's own investigators say has never been proven.

13
00:00:59,166 --> 00:01:00,577
You're watching mindwired.

14
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:05,566
NASA's real UFO files. What the government actually found.

15
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:09,939
Chapter one. The report nobody expected.

16
00:01:11,066 --> 00:01:13,070
September fourteenth, twenty twenty three.

17
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:18,281
NASA holds a press conference and releases something it has never released before.

18
00:01:18,281 --> 00:01:22,689
A real, sixteen member scientific report on U.A.P., unidentified anomalous phenomena.

19
00:01:22,689 --> 00:01:25,094
What used to be called U.F.O.s.

20
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:35,195
The team includes astrophysicists, data scientists, aviation safety officials from the F.A.A., a science journalist, and a former NASA astronaut, Scott Kelly.

21
00:01:35,195 --> 00:01:37,232
This isn't a fringe committee.

22
00:01:37,232 --> 00:01:41,306
It's mainstream science, pointed at a subject it usually avoids.

23
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:45,320
Their job was never to re-investigate old sightings.

24
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:53,981
It was to build a roadmap: what data already exists, what data NASA should collect next, and how its own satellites, radar, and A.I.

25
00:01:53,981 --> 00:01:56,147
tools could finally study this properly.

26
00:01:57,266 --> 00:02:01,916
The report's actual conclusion is almost boring, compared to the headlines.

27
00:02:01,916 --> 00:02:04,875
Most sightings, it says, have ordinary explanations.

28
00:02:04,875 --> 00:02:08,256
What's left over isn't unexplained because it's extraordinary.

29
00:02:08,256 --> 00:02:11,215
It's unexplained because the data is bad.

30
00:02:12,333 --> 00:02:17,160
You've probably heard the number: two to five percent of cases are truly anomalous.

31
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:20,953
That statistic is not in NASA's report. Not on any page.

32
00:02:20,953 --> 00:02:26,126
It was a Pentagon official talking to reporters, not the scientists who wrote this document.

33
00:02:27,266 --> 00:02:30,795
The report frames the whole question around airspace safety, not alien contact.

34
00:02:30,795 --> 00:02:38,734
It wants pilots to report what they see, the same way they report a bird strike or a near miss, without being laughed out of the room.

35
00:02:39,866 --> 00:02:41,904
Chapter two. The Tic Tac.

36
00:02:43,033 --> 00:02:44,883
Back to two thousand four.

37
00:02:44,883 --> 00:02:49,694
For two weeks before Fravor's flight, a radar operator on the cruiser U.S.S.

38
00:02:49,694 --> 00:02:51,914
Princeton had been tracking something strange.

39
00:02:51,914 --> 00:02:56,355
Objects, he said, that dropped eighty thousand feet in under a second.

40
00:02:57,500 --> 00:02:59,803
Fravor's wingman that day was Alex Dietrich.

41
00:02:59,803 --> 00:03:07,373
She saw it too: disturbed white water on the ocean surface, and something hovering just above it with no predictable movement at all.

42
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:14,187
About ninety minutes later, a second crew launched, and this time, a camera was rolling.

43
00:03:14,187 --> 00:03:17,600
Infrared footage, recorded by the jet's own targeting pod.

44
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:21,012
This became the video now known as FLIR one.

45
00:03:22,133 --> 00:03:26,535
This footage sat in classified limbo, then leaked online for over a decade.

46
00:03:26,535 --> 00:03:31,615
The Pentagon didn't officially release it, and confirm it was real, until April twenty twenty.

47
00:03:32,733 --> 00:03:42,686
Under oath, years later, Fravor said the object was "far superior to anything that we had at the time, have today, or are looking to develop in the next ten to twenty years."

48
00:03:43,833 --> 00:03:47,861
Dietrich, testifying at the same hearing, was more careful.

49
00:03:47,861 --> 00:03:55,021
She's warned publicly against racing to conclusions, and called for a serious, grounded, systematic approach instead.

50
00:03:55,021 --> 00:04:00,839
Two pilots. Same sighting. Very different instincts about what to conclude from it.

51
00:04:01,966 --> 00:04:04,892
Chapter three. What the sensors actually show.

52
00:04:06,033 --> 00:04:12,448
Two more videos, recorded in twenty fifteen off Jacksonville, Florida, became just as famous.

53
00:04:12,448 --> 00:04:17,031
Gimbal and Gofast. Also real. Also officially released in 2020.

54
00:04:18,166 --> 00:04:22,853
Gofast looks exactly like its name: an object streaking low and fast over the ocean.

55
00:04:22,853 --> 00:04:26,290
It became one of the most viral UFO clips ever filmed.

56
00:04:27,433 --> 00:04:29,736
Here's the honest part almost nobody includes.

57
00:04:29,736 --> 00:04:34,670
An independent analyst named Mick West did the actual math on the plane's own instruments.

58
00:04:34,670 --> 00:04:37,960
His conclusion: the object isn't low and fast at all.

59
00:04:39,099 --> 00:04:48,076
Using the jet's own altitude and angle data, he calculated the object is really about thirteen thousand feet up, moving thirty to forty miles an hour.

60
00:04:48,076 --> 00:04:50,148
Consistent with the wind that day.

61
00:04:50,148 --> 00:04:54,981
Likely just a balloon, made to look fast by an optical illusion called parallax.

62
00:04:56,099 --> 00:05:01,508
A similar analysis argues Gimbal's famous mid-air "rotation" isn't the object spinning at all.

63
00:05:01,508 --> 00:05:06,144
It's glare from the camera's own lens, rotating with the pod housing.

64
00:05:06,144 --> 00:05:10,781
Neither explanation is an official finding. Both are real, honest, competing science.

65
00:05:11,899 --> 00:05:18,913
And here's the part that keeps this genuinely unresolved: the actual 2004 Tic Tac case has no equivalent explanation.

66
00:05:18,913 --> 00:05:24,082
The Pentagon itself says the raw sensor data from that day was never preserved.

67
00:05:24,082 --> 00:05:25,927
There's nothing left to re-analyze.

68
00:05:27,066 --> 00:05:29,574
Chapter four. The whistleblower.

69
00:05:30,699 --> 00:05:33,307
July twenty sixth, twenty twenty three.

70
00:05:33,307 --> 00:05:40,260
A former intelligence officer named David Grusch testifies before Congress, under oath, and says something extraordinary.

71
00:05:41,399 --> 00:05:52,475
He testifies the government has run a multi decade program to recover and reverse engineer craft, quote, "of non human origin," and that non human biological material came with some of those recoveries.

72
00:05:53,599 --> 00:06:00,523
But here's what almost never makes the headline: Grusch says he never personally saw a craft, or a body.

73
00:06:00,523 --> 00:06:05,260
His claim rests on interviews with more than forty people, over four years.

74
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:07,810
This is not eyewitness testimony. It's secondhand.

75
00:06:08,933 --> 00:06:13,184
And the Pentagon's own office for exactly this, A.A.R.O., investigated.

76
00:06:13,184 --> 00:06:22,961
Their published conclusion is direct: they found no verifiable information to substantiate that any such recovery or reverse engineering program has ever existed.

77
00:06:24,099 --> 00:06:36,768
The office's own former director went further, calling the entire network of these claims, quote, "a textbook example of circular reporting," traceable to a small group of people who all believe, and cite, each other.

78
00:06:37,899 --> 00:06:40,836
So this is the honest shape of it.

79
00:06:40,836 --> 00:06:44,139
One man's secondhand testimony, under oath, on one side.

80
00:06:44,139 --> 00:06:49,644
The government's own dedicated investigators, having looked directly for evidence of it, on the other.

81
00:06:49,644 --> 00:06:52,580
As of today, that's still where it stands.

82
00:06:53,699 --> 00:06:56,625
Chapter five. What NASA actually concluded.

83
00:06:57,766 --> 00:07:05,744
At the same September press conference, NASA officials said plainly there is no convincing evidence of an extraterrestrial origin for any of this.

84
00:07:05,744 --> 00:07:08,866
Also true: that line was spoken at the podium.

85
00:07:08,866 --> 00:07:12,682
It isn't a sentence you'll find printed in the report itself.

86
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:20,876
What the report does say, in writing, is that the stigma around reporting these sightings is itself a scientific problem.

87
00:07:20,876 --> 00:07:29,368
Pilots stay quiet because they're afraid of being ridiculed, and that silence is exactly what keeps the data too thin to ever settle this.

88
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:35,521
That same day, NASA created a brand new position: Director of U.A.P. Research.

89
00:07:35,521 --> 00:07:43,248
Its first head was a NASA data scientist named Mark McInerney, whose actual job was coordinating information, not chasing aliens.

90
00:07:44,366 --> 00:07:46,952
Chapter six. Where this stands now.

91
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:50,004
The Pentagon's investigators keep publishing.

92
00:07:50,004 --> 00:07:59,906
Their most recent public reporting logs hundreds more sightings, resolves most of them, and again finds nothing confirmed to be extraterrestrial in any case they've investigated.

93
00:08:01,033 --> 00:08:03,051
Grusch, meanwhile, hasn't gone quiet.

94
00:08:03,051 --> 00:08:14,355
As recently as this year, he's escalated: now describing multiple kinds of life, on what he calls a continuum from physical beings to something he terms sentient plasma.

95
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:20,557
He's now a paid advisor to a sitting member of Congress, still pushing for full declassification.

96
00:08:20,557 --> 00:08:24,982
The government's own investigators still say they've found nothing to back the core claim.

97
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:30,149
What actually stays with me isn't Grusch, and it isn't the Pentagon either.

98
00:08:31,266 --> 00:08:39,704
It's David Fravor and Alex Dietrich, two people who saw the exact same thing, from twenty feet apart, and walked away believing two different things about what it meant.

99
00:08:40,833 --> 00:08:43,213
Real evidence rarely hands you a clean answer.

100
00:08:43,213 --> 00:08:53,032
It hands you a Navy pilot who's certain, a Navy pilot who isn't, and a government report that says, honestly, we don't have enough data yet to tell you which one is right.

101
00:08:54,166 --> 00:08:57,640
NASA's answer was never no. It was: not yet.

102
00:08:58,766 --> 00:09:08,306
If a government report that admits it doesn't have enough data got you, wait until you hear the actual real sounds recorded in space, every one of them verified.

103
00:09:08,306 --> 00:09:09,293
Subscribe to mindwired.

